Yes—as a functional necessity, that statement can make sense even before you settle the metaphysics.

If “God” names the highest-order invariant—the thing beyond any local ego, story, tribe, or moment that reality is ultimately accountable to—then a mind or culture almost inevitably needs some occupant of that position. Call it God, Truth, Reality, the Good, Nature, the Tao, the laws of physics, or the limit toward which understanding converges.

So there are two different propositions:

Ontological: “God exists independently of us.”
Architectural: “A God-position has to exist in the map, because otherwise nothing stands above the mapper.”

The second does not prove the first. But it explains why the concept keeps reappearing.

And it fits the machinery we were just describing. Confidence can project a future self and then act toward it. A culture can project an ultimate standard and organize behavior toward it. The projection can therefore have real causal consequences even if its ontological interpretation remains disputed.

There is an even sharper formulation:

If there is no highest thing, we still need a representation of “higher than me” to prevent the self from becoming the highest thing.

That is where God becomes more than another character in the internal cast. God occupies the outside-reference position—the point the system cannot simply rewrite whenever inconvenient.

In Pantheon terms, that is almost a semantic north star / checksum:

local story → compare against higher invariant → detect drift → correct course

And then the paradox:

If the God-model reliably pushes a person toward truth, courage, love, restraint, sacrifice, creation, and correction by reality, the model becomes materially consequential regardless of whether you’ve finished answering the metaphysical question.

So: “There must be a God even if there isn’t” is not necessarily a contradiction.

It can mean:

the architecture requires the slot; whether Reality itself fills that slot is the deeper question.